  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "judge classifications differ",
      "resolution": "The disagreement concerns evidentiary sufficiency, not incompatible reconstructions of the update. Measure normalization is resolved by the supplement. The supplied evidence does not comparably characterize the opaque integral on integrable fields, so unconditional semantic acceptance remains unsupported.",
      "primary_evidence": "Both judgments reconstruct the same signed update and conditional bound. D101 exposes only MeasureTheory.wrapped✝.1; the supplement expands Real.measureSpace and states interval-volume identities but supplies no corresponding defining characterization of MeasureTheory.integral."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "Adjudication resolves the measure and normalization objections and distinguishes irrelevant exceptional behavior from the remaining integral-identification gap. The request is therefore substantively addressed without adopting either judge's verdict by authority.",
      "primary_evidence": "Native output explicitly identifies volume with StieltjesFunction.id.measure and states Real.volume_Ioc. D020 supplies interval integrability, while D101 remains an opaque projection."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "Retain undetermined, with a narrower reason: the physical interpretation of the integral on its actual integrable domain remains insufficiently exposed. Neither a definite mismatch nor complete equivalence follows from the supplied declaration evidence.",
      "primary_evidence": "The exact target and D001-D004 establish the algebraic construction, but all physical reference averages depend on D101. The native supplement contains no expansion or characterization resolving that operator."
    },
    {
      "item": "blind translator left dependency_coverage item D061 unclear",
      "resolution": "Resolved. The selected real measure has ordinary length normalization on the Borel real line; it is not an unspecified or arbitrarily scaled measure.",
      "primary_evidence": "The supplement expands Real.measureSpace through measureSpaceOfInnerProductSpace, identifies its measurable structure with borel Real, states Real.volume_eq_stieltjes_id, and explicitly states volume (Set.Ioc a b) = ENNReal.ofReal (b-a). These declarations identify the same Real.measureSpace used by the target."
    },
    {
      "item": "blind translator left dependency_coverage item D101 unclear",
      "resolution": "Partly resolved but materially open. Integrability premises remove the need to inspect nonintegrable fallback behavior. They do not establish what the opaque operator computes for integrable fields. The supplied type, wrapper projection, module provenance, and local documentation identify the intended Bochner operator but do not independently expose its defining analytic meaning.",
      "primary_evidence": "D101's entire displayed body is MeasureTheory.wrapped✝.1. D079 constructs intervalIntegral from this operator. The supplied average-congruence and constant-average declarations give some properties, but no general characterization by the standard integral or its construction is supplied. Runtime hashes establish provenance rather than that missing characterization."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C05 unclear",
      "resolution": "The expected local conclusion clauses are present under Q7: normalized averages, a numerical update, an exact error identity, and independent input bounds controlling the next error. No separate global PDE or classical-discontinuity theorem is required for this row. Identification of the reference averages with physical integrals remains conditional on D101.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 5, Section 1.2 describes average data and edge-flux updates. Q7 selects conditional error propagation. The target's final two conjuncts state the weighted error identity and its norm bound."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C06 unclear",
      "resolution": "D001-D004 explicitly preserve average normalization and the update operation; D020, D070, and D079 preserve interval domains and orientation. Arithmetic projections preserve coordinatewise real operations. D061 is resolved by native declarations. C06 remains unclear solely insofar as D101's analytic meaning is not exposed; no local-definition mismatch was found.",
      "primary_evidence": "D004 is reciprocal length times intervalIntegral; D002 is reciprocal converted region measure times restricted integral; D003 is oldAverage minus timeStep/cellVolume times netOutwardFlux. D040-D041 explicitly define pointwise addition and subtraction."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
      "resolution": "Positive finite interval measures exclude zero or infinite normalization denominators, and the four integrability premises exclude nonintegrable inputs. The theorem imposes no classical smoothness. Its boundary histories are specifically flux(q(a,τ)) and flux(q(b,τ)); it does not establish invariance under arbitrary modifications on spatial faces or supply canonical traces. Those broader assertions must not be inferred.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target assumes a<b, s<t, four IntervalIntegrable predicates, and balance for the selected rectangle. The supplement states Real.volume_Ioc. Printed pages 4-5 retain integral conservation at discontinuities, while the inherited receipt expressly does not settle arbitrary pointwise representative conventions."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C12 unclear",
      "resolution": "No contradictory premise, empty-coordinate trick, or forced exact numerical solution is present. Under ordinary integral semantics, m=1, unit intervals, zero physical state and flux, zero old numerical value, and numerical left/right fluxes 0 and 1 give next error 1 with bounds 0,0,1. Thus the intended claim is nonvacuous. This example does not independently characterize D101.",
      "primary_evidence": "D003 gives the update -1 in that example, and the target permits distinct face labels and arbitrary rules. The supplied native consumer declarations also state a nonzero error value of 1, but their auxiliary field definition is absent, so they are not used as a complete independent witness."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N02 unclear",
      "resolution": "The norm is exactly the finite Pi norm selected in the elaborated target. Its maximum-coordinate formula is not derived by the displayed bodies, so the direct judge's explicit formula exceeds the exposed evidence. This does not itself block the interpreted estimate: Q7 specifies no coordinate norm, and the weighted-error estimate works for any compatible real normed-space structure. No Euclidean, spatial, squared, or operator norm is substituted.",
      "primary_evidence": "D030 and D035 select the norm from D044; D044 inherits it from Pi.seminormedRing. D042-D045 expose finite-product normed structures and pointwise scalar action. The proof-free norm_le_of_weighted_error_balance declaration is generic over NormedAddCommGroup and NormedSpace Real."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "The implication is structurally correct under Q7 and the applicable local rectangle interpretation, provided the reference integral is the ordinary Bochner integral. The unresolved identification of D101 prevents an unqualified yes verdict on the supplied evidence.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target uses numericalOld as rule input, compares its face outputs with time averages of flux(q), and bounds the error of the same update against the new spatial average. These are the selected interpretation's required roles."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear",
      "resolution": "Ordinary rectangle conservation, normalized averages, and Q7's time-averaged-flux interpretation yield every substantive target identity and bound by algebra and the triangle inequality. The missing bridge to the opaque integral prevents completing this implication for the exact Lean proposition.",
      "primary_evidence": "Equation (1.10) has left-minus-right physical flux. With h=b-a and k=t-s, rectangle conservation gives h(A_t-A_s)=k(F_L-F_R). D003 defines U_new=U_old+(k/h)(G_L-G_R), yielding the target error identity and bound."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded coordinator-selected Q7 interpretation and the inherited user's rectangle convention only within its applicable conservation scope, the target has the required local data flow, signs, normalization factors, and conditional error propagation. It neither requires global solutionhood nor claims unconditional accuracy. However, the supplied declaration evidence does not independently characterize D101 on integrable fields, so the exact reference quantities cannot yet be fully identified with physical cell and face-flux integrals. The normalized real measure itself is resolved."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under those same recorded interpretations, the ordinary physical balance gives h(A_t-A_s)=k(F_L-F_R). Combining it with the selected conservative update gives h(U_new-A_t)=h(U_old-A_s)+k((G_L-F_L)-(G_R-F_R)); positivity and norm inequalities give the stated bound. Average certificates and region normalizations follow from integrability and interval volume. This derivation establishes the intended mathematical proposition, but its identification with the exact opaque integral remains unresolved. The printed paragraph alone does not prescribe this recurrence or quantitative theorem."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "D101 needs a proof-free defining expansion or sufficient native characterization identifying its values on integrable finite real-vector fields with the standard Bochner integral. Its exceptional nonintegrable behavior is not needed for this target.",
    "The displayed Pi-instance bodies do not establish a maximum-coordinate formula. This is a nonblocking descriptive limitation because the selected interpretation prescribes no particular coordinate norm and the estimate is norm-generic.",
    "The printed source leaves temporal flux normalization, quantitative accuracy, and discontinuity trace conventions implicit. Q7 and the inherited receipt remain separate, scoped interpretations rather than printed-source assertions."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-integral-identification",
      "evidence": "D101 exposes only MeasureTheory.wrapped✝.1. The native supplement resolves measure normalization but provides no general characterization of this integral on the integrable fields used by the target.",
      "impact": "Both implication directions remain unclear. This is an evidence gap, not a demonstrated incorrect formalization."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "measure-objection-resolved",
      "evidence": "The supplement explicitly identifies the selected volume with the identity Stieltjes measure and gives volume (Set.Ioc a b) = ENNReal.ofReal (b-a).",
      "impact": "Arbitrary measure scaling and zero/infinite interval-volume concerns do not remain blockers."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "interpretation-and-strength",
      "evidence": "The source paragraph is descriptive; Q7 separately selects independent input error bounds. The target assumes local conservation and derives a local update estimate without classical regularity.",
      "impact": "Any later acceptance must be interpretation-qualified. Neither extra certificates nor assumed conservation justify a faithful-stronger classification; no genuine strict strengthening has been established."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "I inspected the attached primary-source pages and exact inline declarations without tools, proofs, or independent hash recomputation. The numerical construction matches the recorded interpretation at the algebraic and object-role levels. Native declarations resolve the disputed real measure, and positive intervals and explicit integrability address the relevant exceptional cases. The norm's unexpanded coordinate formula is unnecessary for this norm-generic claim. The remaining integral-identification gap is material because every physical reference quantity depends on it; provenance and integrability do not supply its missing analytic characterization. Accordingly, uncertainty is preserved rather than converting the direct judge's standard-library interpretation into independently established declaration evidence."
